          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should oil absorbent sit on a spill?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "At least an hour on a small fresh spill, and overnight on anything larger than about a cup. Grind the granules into the concrete before leaving them. Sweep up, and if the floor still looks wet or dark and shiny, apply fresh absorbent and repeat before washing with a degreaser."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between oil-only and universal absorbents?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Oil-only absorbents, usually white pads and treated cellulose, take up oil and fuel but repel water, so they work outdoors and float. Universal absorbents, including clay, diatomaceous earth and grey or blue pads and socks, take up oil, coolant, brake fluid and water. Universal is best for a dry garage; oil-only for wet surfaces."
