        <div id="which-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Oil Absorbent for Which Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oil absorbents come in three physical forms, and the form matters more than the brand. Loose granules, the stuff most people call oil dry, are poured onto a spill, left to soak, and swept up. Pads, rolls and socks are sheets and tubes of polypropylene or filled fabric that you lay down, let saturate, and lift. Drip mats are reusable or semi-disposable sheets that live under a parked vehicle and catch oil before it ever reaches the concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each form suits a different moment. Granules are the best tool for a spill that has already spread on a porous floor, because they press into the texture of the concrete and pull oil out of the surface pores. Pads are faster and cleaner for a puddle or a pool under a drain plug: drop, blot, lift, no sweeping. Socks are for keeping a spill from going anywhere, such as across the floor toward the driveway or down a drain. Mats are prevention, and for a car with a slow leak they are the single most useful thing on this page.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide is about catching oil before it becomes a stain. Once it has soaked in and set, you are into cleaning and stain removal, which our guides to the <a href="/best-concrete-degreaser/">best concrete degreasers</a> and <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a> cover. And if you want a full-bay floor covering rather than a mat under the engine, our <a href="/best-garage-floor-mat/">garage floor mat guide</a> covers containment mats and parking mats.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
