        <div id="clay" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Clay Oil Dry: Cheap, Heavy and Good Enough
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clay granules are the classic shop floor absorbent and still the cheapest per pound. Most are made from naturally porous clays such as montmorillonite, which is what Oil-Dri uses in its premium absorbents. The granules hold liquid in their internal pores and in the gaps between them, and they are heavy enough to stay put when you sweep around them or open the garage door on a windy day.
          </p>

          <p>
            The downsides are weight and volume. A 40 pound bag is a lot to carry for a garage that sees one spill a year, and clay absorbs less per pound than diatomaceous earth or cellulose, so you use more of it and sweep up more of it. Clay also absorbs everything, water included, which means it turns to a gritty mud on a wet driveway and gets heavier and messier to dispose of. Grind it underfoot and it breaks into fine dust that can scuff a coated floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Used properly, clay is good enough for most home spills. The trick most people skip is grinding it in. After pouring a layer about half an inch thick over the spill, step on it and twist your shoe, or work it with a stiff broom, so the granules press into the surface of the concrete. That contact is what pulls oil out of the pores rather than just off the top. Leave it for at least an hour on a fresh spill, overnight on a heavy one, then sweep it up and repeat if the floor still looks wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plain, non-clumping clay cat litter is essentially the same material and works in a pinch. Clumping litter is designed to bind water into lumps and costs more per pound, so it is not the better choice for oil. Whatever you use, wear a dust mask when pouring and sweeping in a closed garage; any fine mineral dust is worth keeping out of your lungs, and some clay and diatomite absorbents carry silica warnings on their safety data sheets.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="de-cellulose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Diatomaceous Earth and Cellulose: Lighter and Thirstier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Diatomaceous earth, often sold as floor dry, is the fossilised remains of microscopic algae with a skeleton full of tiny pores. Granular DE absorbents are lighter than clay for the same volume and generally absorb more per pound, which is the whole case for them: fewer bags to carry, less material to sweep up and less waste to throw away. The EP Minerals 25 pound bag on this page is rated by its listing at 9 quarts of liquid absorbed, a figure you can actually plan around.
          </p>

          <p>
            Like clay, DE is a universal absorbent. It takes oil, coolant and water equally, which makes it a good general-purpose shelf product for a garage where the next spill might be antifreeze rather than oil. It shares clay's dust problem, and food-grade DE sold for pest control is a much finer powder that is not a good spill absorbent; buy the granular industrial product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cellulose absorbents are a third option, made from recycled paper or plant fibre and often treated to repel water. PIG Lite-Dri, the oil-only cellulose product here, is described by New Pig as silica-free, hydrophobic and light enough to float, with a 22 pound bag rated to absorb up to 8 gallons of oil-based liquid. That makes it the strongest loose absorbent on this page by weight, and the only one that will sit on a puddle of rainwater and take just the oil sheen off the top.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cost per bag is higher for both DE and cellulose than for clay, but the cost per gallon absorbed is often close or better, and you carry and dispose of far less weight. For a home garage that buys one bag every few years, a lighter, thirstier absorbent is usually the better buy. For a busy shop floor that goes through bags weekly, clay remains the economical default.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pads-socks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Polypropylene Pads, Rolls and Socks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pads are sheets of melt-blown polypropylene, the same family of material used in many filters and masks. Polypropylene is naturally oleophilic and hydrophobic: it attracts oil and resists water, which is why white oil-only pads are the standard tool for marine and industrial spills. They are clean to handle, quick to deploy and lift away in one piece, with nothing to sweep.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight matters. Heavyweight pads have more material per square foot and hold more oil before they drip when you lift them. The PIG MAT403 pads here are heavyweight 15 by 20 inch sheets rated up to 28 ounces per pad and about 22 gallons per bag of 100. Brady SPC BPO100 pads are 15 by 17 inches, rated at 20.5 gallons per pack of 100, and are listed as dimpled and perforated so you can tear them to size. For an oil change, a single heavyweight pad under the drain plug and filter catches the drips that miss the pan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pads have limits on concrete. They absorb what they touch, and on a rough slab they touch only the high points, so oil that has already reached the pores is better pulled out with granules pressed into the texture. Use pads to soak up a pool quickly, then put granules on the shadow left behind. Pads are also the better choice on epoxy or sealed floors, where there are no pores to clean out and the job is just lifting a puddle off a smooth surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Socks are tubes of fabric filled with absorbent, and their job is containment. Laid around a spill they form a flexible dam that stops it spreading, then absorb what reaches them. The PIG Blue sock, model PIG238, is 3 inches by 4 feet and rated to absorb up to 95 ounces per sock, and New Pig describes it as its heaviest absorbent sock. At home, socks earn their place across the garage door threshold during a fluid change, around a leaking appliance, or across a storm drain while you wash the driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oil-only" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Oil-Only vs Universal: The Water Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every absorbent is either oil-only or universal, and this is the one specification worth reading on the bag. Oil-only absorbents, usually white pads and treated cellulose, take up petroleum products and repel water. Universal absorbents, usually grey or blue pads, clay and DE, take up everything: oil, coolant, brake fluid, water and most mild chemicals.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil-only is the right choice when water is present. On a driveway after rain, or when washing down a spill, a universal absorbent fills up with water and has little capacity left for oil. An oil-only pad or granule ignores the water and takes just the oil. That is why oil-only products float, and why they are the standard for boats and outdoor spills. The PIG MAT403 and Brady BPO100 pads and PIG Lite-Dri are all oil-only.
          </p>

          <p>
            Universal is the right choice for a dry garage where the next spill could be anything. Antifreeze and windshield fluid are water-based, and an oil-only pad will not touch them. Antifreeze in particular should be cleaned up fully and quickly, because ethylene glycol is toxic and pets are attracted to its taste. The Oil-Dri clay, EP Minerals DE and PIG Blue sock here are all universal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical answer for most garages is to keep one of each: a bag of universal granules for anything that hits a dry floor, and a pack of oil-only pads for oil changes and anything outdoors. Together they cost less than one professional stain treatment, and they cover every spill a household is likely to have.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Wet surface or outdoors: oil-only. Dry garage and unknown fluid: universal.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Oil-only absorbents repel water and float. Universal absorbents take coolant and brake fluid too, but fill up with rainwater.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="fresh-spill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Fresh Spill: The First Ten Minutes
          </h2>

          <p>
            How you handle the first few minutes decides whether you are left with a clean floor or a permanent shadow. Oil soaks into unsealed concrete quickly, and the longer it sits, the deeper it goes. The goal is to get as much oil as possible into an absorbent before it gets into the slab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step one is to stop the source and contain the edge. Set the dripping part on a pad or in a pan, and if the puddle is spreading, put a sock or a ring of granules around it. Step two is to lift the pool. If there is standing oil, lay pads on it and press them down; they will take up most of the free liquid in seconds without spreading it. Do not wipe with rags, which smear oil into a wider area.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step three is to treat the shadow. Once the pool is gone, the concrete underneath will look dark and wet. Cover it with granules about half an inch deep, grind them in with your shoe or a stiff broom, and leave them. An hour is the minimum for a small spill; overnight is better for anything over a cup. Sweep up and look: if the floor still looks wet, repeat with fresh granules.
          </p>

          <p>
            Step four is a wash, and only after the absorbent has done its work. Scrub the remaining mark with a water-based alkaline degreaser, let it dwell, and rinse. The methods in <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">how to get oil out of concrete</a> go into this in more detail. Do not hose a fresh spill down first; water spreads oil across the floor and toward the gutter, and turns clay granules into mud.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gasoline is the exception to the patience rule. It evaporates quickly and its vapour is the hazard. Ventilate the garage, kill any ignition sources such as a water heater pilot, use pads or granules to take up what remains, and move the used absorbent outside into a closed metal container right away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="slow-drip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. A Slow Drip: Drip Mats and Containment
          </h2>

          <p>
            A car that leaves a few drops of oil every night is a different problem from a spill, and granules are the wrong answer. Pouring oil dry under a parked car every week is messy, gets tracked into the house and does nothing about the oil that lands between applications. The right tool is something under the car that catches every drop before it reaches the concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            A drip mat is the simplest. The Drymate Oil Spill Mat on this page is a 29 by 36 inch sheet with an absorbent top layer and a waterproof backing, made in the USA and sold as reusable and washable. Slide it under the engine or transmission, where the drip lands, and it holds the oil while the backing stops it soaking through to the floor. Drymate also sells larger 36 by 59 inch and jumbo sizes if the leak is wandering or you have more than one problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            A cheaper option for short-term use is a heavyweight oil-only pad, taped down or weighed at the corners, under the drip. It works, but it needs replacing as it saturates and it shifts when you drive over it. A pad is a good stopgap the night you notice a leak; a proper mat is the better answer if the repair is weeks away.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a whole parking bay, containment mats with raised edges catch snowmelt, salt water and oil together. They are a bigger investment and a different product category, covered in our <a href="/best-garage-floor-mat/">garage floor mat guide</a>. And remember that a drip mat is a holding action. A car that drips oil every night is losing oil every night, and the fix is a gasket or a drain plug washer, not a better mat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="disposal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Disposal: What You Can and Cannot Bin
          </h2>

          <p>
            Used absorbent is the part of an oil spill most people never think about, and the rules vary by town, so the honest answer starts with checking your local solid waste or household hazardous waste program. That said, a few principles hold almost everywhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recover free oil before it becomes absorbent waste. If you can pour or pump oil out of a drain pan or catch basin, take it to a used oil collection point; many auto parts stores accept used motor oil from households. Absorbent should be for what you cannot recover, not a way to throw away a quart of oil in the trash.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many localities allow small amounts of oil-soaked absorbent from a household spill in regular trash, provided it contains no free liquid, meaning nothing drips or squeezes out. Bag it securely. Others ask you to take it to a household hazardous waste drop-off. Absorbent used on gasoline, solvents or antifreeze is more likely to be classed as hazardous, and gasoline-soaked material is a fire risk, so let your local program decide and store it outdoors in a closed metal can until then.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reusable drip mats are the easiest case: the makers sell them as washable, and the Drymate mat can be cleaned and returned to service. Wring or blot the excess onto a pad first so the oil goes into the trash bag rather than down the drain, and replace the mat when it no longer absorbs. Never wash oily mats or pads in a household washing machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When the Stain Stays: What Comes After the Absorbent
          </h2>

          <p>
            Absorbents are very good at free oil and oil in the top layer of concrete. They are much weaker on oil that soaked in days or months ago, because there is no liquid left near the surface for them to draw up. If you have swept up two rounds of granules and the concrete is still dark, you have moved from spill cleanup to stain removal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The next step is a wash with a proper concrete degreaser, which removes the oily surface film and the dirt it holds and often makes a stain look much lighter. Our <a href="/best-concrete-degreaser/">concrete degreaser guide</a> covers dilution, dwell time and pressure washer use. If that does not finish the job, a poultice, which is an absorbent mixed with a solvent and left to dry on the stain, or a microbial treatment that digests the oil over weeks, is the right tool. Both are covered in our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from a driveway</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the floor is clean, think about sealing it. A penetrating sealer slows how fast oil soaks into concrete, which turns the next spill from a race into an easy wipe-up. Our guide to the <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">best penetrating concrete sealers</a> explains which products resist oil and how to apply them to a garage floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then restock the shelf. The absorbents that worked this time are the ones that need to be in the garage next time, and a half-used bag of granules and a few pads are worth far more sitting by the door than in the trunk on the way home from the store.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Hosing down a fresh spill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water spreads oil across the floor and toward the gutter, turns clay into mud and leaves universal absorbents full of water. Absorb first, with pads on the pool and granules on the shadow, and wash only once the absorbent has taken up everything it can.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sprinkling granules without grinding them in</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Loose granules sitting on top of a spill only touch the oil on the surface. Step on them and twist, or work them with a stiff broom, so they press into the concrete texture and pull oil out of the pores. Then give them time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using oil-only pads on antifreeze</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oil-only absorbents repel water-based fluids, and antifreeze, washer fluid and many coolants are water-based. Keep a universal absorbent for those, and clean antifreeze up quickly and completely because ethylene glycol is toxic to pets.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring oil dry under a leaking car every week</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It gets tracked into the house, misses the drops that land between applications and builds a crust of oily grit. A drip mat under the leak catches everything, and a fixed gasket stops the problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Throwing oil-soaked absorbent away carelessly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Recover free oil for recycling first, bag absorbent with no free liquid, and follow your local rules. Gasoline or solvent-soaked material is a fire risk and often counts as household hazardous waste; store it outside in a closed metal can.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best oil absorbent for concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a fresh spill on bare concrete, use both: pads to lift the standing oil quickly, then granules ground into the surface to pull oil out of the pores. PIG MAT403 pads and EP Minerals diatomaceous earth granules make a strong pair. On a wet driveway, use an oil-only product such as PIG Lite-Dri, which repels water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is kitty litter as good as oil dry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plain, non-clumping clay litter is close to clay oil dry and works in an emergency. Clumping litter is designed for water, costs more per pound and is not better for oil. Both are less absorbent per pound than diatomaceous earth or cellulose products, so you will use more and sweep up more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should oil absorbent sit on a spill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At least an hour on a small fresh spill, and overnight on anything larger than about a cup. Grind the granules into the concrete before leaving them. Sweep up, and if the floor still looks wet or dark and shiny, apply fresh absorbent and repeat before washing with a degreaser.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between oil-only and universal absorbents?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Oil-only absorbents, usually white pads and treated cellulose, take up oil and fuel but repel water, so they work outdoors and float. Universal absorbents, including clay, diatomaceous earth and grey or blue pads and socks, take up oil, coolant, brake fluid and water. Universal is best for a dry garage; oil-only for wet surfaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will oil absorbent remove an old oil stain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rarely. Absorbents pull up free oil and oil near the surface, so they are best within hours of a spill. An old stain has little liquid oil left near the surface. Clean it with a concrete degreaser, then use a poultice or a microbial treatment if a mark remains.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I dispose of used oil absorbent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check your local solid waste or household hazardous waste program, because rules vary. Many towns accept small amounts of absorbent from a household oil spill in the trash if it contains no free liquid and is bagged. Recycle recoverable oil at a collection point, and store gasoline-soaked absorbent outdoors in a closed metal container.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do drip mats really work under a leaking car?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for slow leaks. A mat with an absorbent top and waterproof backing catches drips before they reach the concrete, and washable mats such as the Drymate can be cleaned and reused. They do not fix the leak, and a mat needs checking every week or two so it is replaced or washed before it saturates.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
